  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 140 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nAny storms are expected to have ended by sunrise Thursday morning as \nthe front clears the forecast area stalling just south of Iowa. \nAfter a short dry period Thursday, this boundary will lift back \nnorthward over the state as low level theta-e advection ensues \nThursday night into Friday ahead of the next southern stream \nshortwave. The shower and storm chances will be prolonged Friday \ninto at least Saturday, though there are likely to be some breaks. \nFurther, will need to monitor diurnal ranges as highs may be too \nhigh and lows too low in the cloudy and damp period. Even if \ntemperatures were lowered now, they would still be seasonal as \ntypical late October highs are in the upper 50s to around 60 \ndegrees. With the southern stream wave lifting over Iowa on Sunday, \nthis may bring some drier conditions back over the state late in the \nweekend. By early next week, a few deterministic models are more \namplified or negatively tilted with a trough approaching the region; \nhowever, ensembles and AI guidance are generally lower amplitude and \nless wound up. The takeaway is that a trough of some variety and \ntilt will be moving through the region early next week and this \nshould spur a continuation of more active weather/shower and storm \nchances Monday into Tuesday. Confidence in any details beyond this \nare low at this time.",
